{
  "$schema": "../../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",

  /* ===========================================================================================
     THE CLOUD DEPLOYMENT — TWO ENVIRONMENTS, ONE LADDER.
     51 §4.6 writes this file in full for a single environment. This is that file with `alpha`
     and `production` split out of it, and the split is not cosmetic: 33 §7 and 31 §8 both say
     plainly *"there is no alpha environment: the ops record is the canary"*, and 53 §10 records
     the ceiling with its own exit — `ponytail: no alpha environment ... add env.alpha in
     wrangler.jsonc when a second person deploys`. This is that day, taken early. The canary
     argument is untouched: the ops record is still the first record to migrate on every deploy
     (53 §5.3). Alpha is not a replacement for it — it is where a DEPLOY is rehearsed, and the
     ops record is where a MIGRATION is. Amend 33 §7, 31 §8 and 53 §13 #14 in the same revision.

     THE FRONTEND IS NOT DEPLOYED SEPARATELY, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THIS FILE. `assets`
     below carries `apps/web/build` INTO the Worker version (33 §7, 50 §13 D2). One `wrangler
     deploy` ships the shell and the code as one version, which is what lets `deploy-parity`
     compare the live `/` against the local `build/` and mean something (52 §3): if they were two
     deploys they could differ for a minute and the gate would be measuring a race. So the answer
     to *"how is the static frontend deployed"* is: it is not, it is a property of the Worker
     version. Only `apps/site` is a separate static deployment, and it has its own file.

     ORDER OF THE KEYS BELOW: everything inheritable first, then the two environments. The
     division is not stylistic — it is the one fact this file has to get right. See the two
     comment blocks at `migrations` and at `env`.
     =========================================================================================== */

  "name": "braindog",
  "main": "src/index.ts",

  /* INHERITABLE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT OVERRIDDEN PER ENVIRONMENT. A alpha environment on a
     different compatibility date is not a rehearsal of production, it is a rehearsal of a
     different runtime — and the one thing this date decides is whether `deleteAll()` also deletes
     the alarm (51 A7, floor 2026-02-24), which is precisely the behaviour a customer deletion
     drill exercises (53 §5.9). One date, both environments, and `compat-date` reads this one key
     (52 §3). */
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",

  /* No `nodejs_compat`: `node:sqlite` is a stub under it (22 §9) and nothing here reaches for
     `node:*`. An empty array rather than an absent key, so a flag added later is a diff. */
  "compatibility_flags": [],

  /* BOTH OFF, IN BOTH ENVIRONMENTS, AND THIS IS A SECURITY SETTING RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
     `workers.dev` and preview URLs are additional ORIGINS serving the same records. 51 §5.1 step 3
     makes every cookie-authenticated mutation require `Origin === "https://" + PUBLIC_HOST`, so a
     second origin is either a surface where nothing can be written (confusing) or, if anyone ever
     "fixes" it by relaxing the check, a CSRF hole with a stable public hostname (12 §8). It is
     also the reason there are no per-pull-request preview deployments — see REPORT.md §4. */
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,

  /* 53 §2.3's five `op` lines are read here. Inheritable, so both environments get it, which is
     what makes a alpha incident debuggable with the same `wrangler tail` the runbook names. */
  "observability": { "enabled": true },

  /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE STATIC SHELL. Inheritable, so both environments serve the same `build/` — there is one
     artefact and it is host-agnostic by construction (50 §2.1, §13 D1/D2).

     `directory` IS RELATIVE TO THIS FILE, which is why it climbs. 51 §4.6 writes
     `apps/worker/src/index.ts` and `apps/web/build` because it imagines the config at the
     repository root; the file actually lives in `apps/worker/`, so both paths shorten by one
     segment and the asset directory gains a `..`. Amend 51 §4.6's two paths, or move the file —
     not both.

     `run_worker_first` IS 33 §4'S LIST AND NOT A SECOND COPY OF IT. Everything outside it is the
     app's (19 §5: *if a future change requires enumerating app paths in two files, that change is
     wrong*) — this enumerates the HOST's paths, which is the complement and is allowed to exist
     once. Several of these routes are not built yet, and listing them anyway is the truthful
     choice: `src/route.ts`'s `servesApi` sends an unbuilt one to `ROUTE_UNKNOWN` (404 JSON),
     whereas leaving it off this list serves `index.html` with a 200 to an agent's POST — a lie
     that looks like a working endpoint. `/.well-known/*` is on the list because 33 §7's skeleton
     has it and `braindog verify` reads `/.well-known/braindog-key` offline (03 §4); the SPA shell
     answering there would break verification of every backup.

     `single-page-application` is what makes `/r/obligations` and `/D41` resolve in the browser
     with no path list on either host (19 §5, 50 §13 D1). */
  "assets": {
    "directory": "../web/build",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
    "run_worker_first": [
      "/api/*",
      "/mcp",
      "/a",
      "/a/*",
      "/in/*",
      "/s/*",
      "/ws",
      "/ws/*",
      "/auth/*",
      "/account/*",
      "/.well-known/*"
    ]
  },

  /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     DURABLE OBJECT MIGRATIONS — THE PART THAT GOES WRONG, SO IT IS THE PART WITH THE LONGEST
     COMMENT. Six facts, in the order they bite:

     1. `migrations` IS AN INHERITABLE KEY, and the migration tag is tracked PER WORKER SCRIPT.
        A named environment is a separate script — `braindog-alpha` and `braindog-production` —
        so each one keeps its own list of applied tags while reading this one ladder. That is
        exactly what is wanted: one ladder, two scripts, each climbing it independently.

     2. THEREFORE THE LADDER LIVES HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. Cloudflare's rule is that a migration
        block inside an environment REPLACES the top-level one; it does not merge. A `migrations`
        key under `env.alpha` is how the two environments silently stop being the same product.
        There is no legitimate reason to write one. If you ever find yourself wanting one, what you
        actually want is a new tag here.

     3. THE ARRAY IS APPEND-ONLY AND EVERY PAST TAG STAYS IN IT FOREVER. Tags are unique names,
        not versions; wrangler applies the ones this script has not applied. Editing an applied tag
        in place is the quietest failure available — the deploy succeeds, the tag is already
        recorded, and nothing runs.

     4. A NEW ENVIRONMENT REPLAYS THE WHOLE LADDER FROM EMPTY. `braindog-alpha` on its first
        deploy applies v1, then v2, then v3, in order, against no data. Every tag must therefore be
        valid against an empty script — which is a live constraint on `renamed_classes`, whose
        `from` class will not exist there. Create alpha BEFORE the first rename lands and the
        problem never arises, because both scripts then walk the same rungs in the same order.
        (Whether a rename of an absent class is a no-op or an error is a *verify* — REPORT.md §8.)

     5. NEVER `deleted_classes`. 12 §8 and 53 §3 both state it as a rule rather than a preference:
        it deletes every object of that class and the data in them, and there is no undo. A class
        that is finished is left bound and unused.

     6. A NEW TAG BLOCKS `wrangler rollback` — Cloudflare refuses a rollback across a Durable
        Object class lifecycle change. This is a SECOND rule on top of 53 §5.2's own (*after a
        kernel migration a rollback is forbidden; every record that migrated answers the old code
        with SCHEMA_NEWER*), and the two are independent: the platform's rule is about the class
        list in this array, the corpus's is about `packages/kernel/migrations/`. Either one alone
        makes the deploy a fix-forward-only deploy.

     THE CLASS NAMES ARE THE CODE'S, NOT 51 §4.6'S. `src/index.ts` exports `Record$` and
     `Directory$`; the `$` is there because `Record` is a TypeScript utility type and the class
     would shadow it. 51 §4.6 writes `Record`, `Spark`, `Directory`. A config naming a class the
     script does not export does not deploy, so this file follows the code and 51 §4.6 is the thing
     to amend. THIS IS FREE TO DECIDE EXACTLY ONCE — before the first production deploy. Afterwards
     the two names cost a `renamed_classes` tag (see REPORT.md §8, risk R2).

     `Spark$` IS NOT HERE BECAUSE THE CLASS IS NOT WRITTEN. 33 §7 lists three classes; the code has
     two. Its rung is drafted below so the shape is settled before it is needed. */
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["Record$", "Directory$"] }

    /* THE NEXT RUNG, WHEN `src/do/spark.ts` LANDS (51 §2, §3). Append; do not edit v1.
       { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["Spark$"] } */

    /* AND IF 51 §4.6'S NAMES WIN AFTER A PRODUCTION DEPLOY, this is the shape — a rename, never
       an edit of v1, and never a delete:
       { "tag": "v3", "renamed_classes": [
           { "from": "Record$", "to": "Record" }, { "from": "Directory$", "to": "Directory" } ] } */
  ],

  /* THE NIGHTLY WALK, 53 §5.1, AND ITS TRIGGER ARRIVED IN ONE CHANGE, which is what the note that
     stood here asked for: a cron pointed at a Worker with no `scheduled()` is a nightly invocation
     that does nothing and says nothing. `src/index.ts` exports one now, over `src/nightly.ts` and
     `src/snapshots.ts`, and it throws when `BLOBS` is unbound rather than returning quietly.

     IT IS AT THE TOP LEVEL RATHER THAN PER-ENVIRONMENT. Inheritable means both environments run the
     walk, and that is the point: 53 §9 wants the drills rehearsed, and a backup path that has only
     ever run in production is a backup path nobody has watched fail. Alpha writes to its own R2
     bucket, so the two nightly runs cannot touch each other.

     BEFORE THE NEXT DEPLOY, THE TWO BUCKETS BELOW HAVE TO EXIST. Binding a bucket that does not
     exist fails the deploy: `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs` and
     `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs-alpha`, once each, on the account. */
  "triggers": { "crons": ["17 3 * * *"] },

  /* ===========================================================================================
     THE TWO ENVIRONMENTS.

     THE ONE RULE: EVERY NON-INHERITABLE KEY IS REPEATED IN FULL IN BOTH BLOCKS. `durable_objects`,
     `queues`, `r2_buckets`, `vars`, `secrets_store_secrets`, `analytics_engine_datasets`,
     `send_email`, `services` and `kv_namespaces` are non-inheritable, and a non-inheritable key
     omitted from an environment is not inherited — it is EMPTY. The deploy succeeds. The Worker
     comes up with no Durable Object binding, or no queue, and the first request is the error
     report. Duplication here is not redundancy, it is the schema.

     `routes` IS INHERITABLE, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME TRAP AND THE MORE DANGEROUS ONE.
     A `routes` key at the top level of this file would be inherited by `env.alpha`, and
     `wrangler deploy --env alpha` would then claim `app.brain.dog`. Two Workers cannot hold one
     Custom Domain, so the likeliest outcome is a failed deploy — but the outcome that is not
     ruled out is alpha answering production's hostname. There is deliberately no top-level
     `routes` key above. Routes are declared here and only here.

     AND THERE IS NO NAMELESS DEPLOY. Because every binding lives inside an environment, a bare
     `wrangler deploy` would upload a third Worker called `braindog` with no bindings at all.
     `deploy.yml` always passes `--env`; do the same by hand. (`wrangler` is expected to refuse
     this outright, since `migrations` names classes the environment does not bind — *verify*.)
     =========================================================================================== */
  "env": {
    /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       ALPHA — script `braindog-alpha`. Deployed on every green merge to master, by nobody.
       Its Durable Objects, its queue, its bucket and its KEK are its own; there is no path by
       which a alpha request reaches a production record, because a DO namespace is scoped to
       the script and these are two scripts.
       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    "alpha": {
      /* TWO LABELS DEEP, AND THE ARGUMENT AGAINST IT WAS TRUE AND IS NOT ANY MORE.
         This said: "one label deep, and that is a TLS constraint rather than a preference.
         Universal SSL on a full-setup zone covers the apex and first-level subdomains only, so
         `app.alpha.brain.dog` has no certificate without Advanced Certificate Manager or Total
         TLS." Correct about Universal SSL, and wrong about this zone: `brain.dog` already carries
         two ACTIVE advanced certificates, one for `*.app.brain.dog` and one for
         `*.alpha.brain.dog`, both managed and both renewing. Checked in the dashboard rather than
         assumed, because the comment it replaces was also written from a general rule.

         So the naming is a naming decision again, and the decision is the obvious one: the
         environment is a label and the surface is a label in front of it.

           brain.dog              app.brain.dog              mcp.brain.dog
           alpha.brain.dog        app.alpha.brain.dog        mcp.alpha.brain.dog

         The apex and `alpha.brain.dog` are the marketing site; `app.*` is this Worker. `mcp.*` is
         reserved and deliberately not claimed here: the MCP transport is not built, and pointing
         that hostname at this script would answer an MCP address with the app's shell, which is the
         shape of lie this repository spends most of its comments hunting. */
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "app.alpha.brain.dog", "custom_domain": true }],

      /* ALPHA'S SHELL IS ITS OWN BUILD, `BRAINDOG_ENV=alpha` into `build-alpha`, so its accent differs
         from production's. An `assets` key inside an environment REPLACES the top-level one rather
         than merging, so every field is repeated: keep it identical to the top level but for the
         directory. */
      "assets": {
        "directory": "../web/build-alpha",
        "binding": "ASSETS",
        "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
        "run_worker_first": [
          "/api/*",
          "/mcp",
          "/a",
          "/a/*",
          "/in/*",
          "/s/*",
          "/ws",
          "/ws/*",
          "/auth/*",
          "/account/*",
          "/.well-known/*"
        ]
      },

      /* `PUBLIC_HOST` IS THE CSRF CHECK. 51 §5.1 step 3 compares `Origin` against
         `https://<PUBLIC_HOST>` on every cookie-authenticated mutation, so a `PUBLIC_HOST` copied
         from production means every write in alpha is a 403 `FORBIDDEN` and every read works —
         a failure that looks like a permissions bug for about an hour.

         `AGE_RECIPIENT` IS A SEPARATE RECIPIENT, not a copy. 12 §8 keeps the age identity outside
         Cloudflare in the operator's password manager; two recipients means a alpha snapshot can
         never be opened with the production identity, and the restore drill (53 §5.5) exercises a
         real key rather than the real key.

         `AI_GATEWAY` is its own gateway so alpha's neuron spend and its rate limits are visible
         apart from production's (12 §8: *per record at AI Gateway*).

         `BRAINDOG_BUILD` IS DECLARED EMPTY AND OVERWRITTEN AT DEPLOY TIME by `--var` in
         `deploy.yml`. It is here so the key exists, so `wrangler types` emits it, and so the
         failure when the workflow forgets it is `""` — which `deploy-parity` catches (52 §13 #17)
         — rather than an undefined binding. It is a var 51 §4.1's `Env` does not list; adding it
         is an amendment, and REPORT.md §8 names why `/api/version` cannot answer without it. */
      "vars": {
        "PUBLIC_HOST": "app.alpha.brain.dog",
        "AI_GATEWAY": "braindog-alpha",
        "AGE_RECIPIENT": "age1<alpha-recipient>",
        "SPARK_ENDPOINT": "",
        "BRAINDOG_BUILD": "",
        /* THE RECORD AN ANONYMOUS GET READS, for the public demo, and empty is none. `demoRecord` in
           `src/route.ts` is the whole of what it opens. It is set to the demo record's `rec_...` id
           once that record exists and holds the seed (`scripts/provision-demo.mjs` says what that
           takes), here or with `--var DEMO_RECORD:<id>` on the deploy. */
        "DEMO_RECORD": "",
        /* EMPTY IS NO SIGN-IN LINKS. The sender, on a domain Email Service sends for, switches them on
           with the `EMAIL` binding below (51 §5.8). */
        "AUTH_MAIL_FROM": "",
        /* AND THE ONE WORD THAT LETS A KEYLESS RECORD BE MADE ON PURPOSE. Empty is the default and
           the default is a refusal: with no `KEK` bound, `/auth/provision` answers
           `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` rather than making a record that can never seal a grant, because a
           record is given its keys once and the moment is the create. `yes` and nothing else turns
           that off, for a host that seals nothing. Set here or with
           `--var PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS:yes` on the deploy, as `DEMO_RECORD` above is set. */
        "PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS": ""
      },

      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [
          { "name": "RECORD", "class_name": "Record$" },
          /* One instance for the whole installation, always `idFromName('directory')`. It is the
             tenant map, and two of them would be two maps that disagree — ADR-061. */
          { "name": "DIRECTORY", "class_name": "Directory$" }
        ]
      },

      /* A QUEUE NAME IS ACCOUNT-GLOBAL, so it carries the environment. Sharing one queue between
         two scripts would have alpha's consumer leasing production's outbox rows — the message
         is `{ record }` and nothing else (01 §2, 51 §13 D16), so the consumer would resolve that
         id against its own namespace, find an empty record, and ack nothing. Quiet, and wrong. */
      "queues": {
        "producers": [{ "queue": "braindog-ingress-alpha", "binding": "INGRESS" }],
        "consumers": [
          {
            "queue": "braindog-ingress-alpha",
            "max_batch_size": 25,
            "max_batch_timeout": 5,
            /* Cloudflare's own retry, over the record's ladder — this one covers a consumer that
               crashed. A delivery that failed is `ackOutbox`, which is the record's, and
               conflating them produces either a retry storm or a job never retried at all. */
            "max_retries": 3
          }
        ]
      },

      /* Outbound email. The record never holds the credential: ADR-062 puts every credential in
         the host. The sending domain is verified in the dashboard, once, per 51 A3. */
      "send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],

      /* THE NIGHTLY COPIES GO HERE, one prefix per record (`<record>/snapshots/`), which is also
         what 51 §5.13 step 3 pages over when a customer is deleted. The bucket must exist before
         this deploy: `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs-alpha`. */
      "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "BLOBS", "bucket_name": "braindog-blobs-alpha" }],

      /* UNCOMMENTED IN THE COMMIT THAT FIRST READS IT, which is this one: `src/inference.ts` is the
         `Inference` port of 33 §1 over `env.AI`, and `src/inference.ts` alone reads this binding.

         THERE IS NO ACCOUNT RESOURCE TO CREATE FIRST, and that is what makes `ai` different from
         every other block below. A queue, a bucket and a Secrets Store store all have to exist
         before a deploy that names them, or the deploy fails. Workers AI is account-level: the
         binding resolves as long as Workers AI is enabled on the account. The three preconditions
         were established off this tree before the commit and are recorded here so the next reader
         does not go looking: Workers AI enabled, credits on the account, `ai (write)` on the deploy
         token. Nothing in this repository can check them, and no test here spends a neuron.

         IT IS NOT INHERITABLE, so it is written out again in `production` below. An omitted
         non-inheritable key is EMPTY rather than inherited — the same trap `r2_buckets` carries.

         `AI_GATEWAY` above is the other half. It names the gateway the calls are routed through, so
         neurons are counted and rate-limited per environment (12 §8), and `collectLog: false` in
         `inference.ts` is what keeps the payloads out of the log (11 §3). */
      "ai": { "binding": "AI" }

      /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         THE REST OF 51 §4.6, DRAFTED AND NOT ACTIVE. Each block below binds a resource the code
         does not yet read. Binding a resource that does not exist fails the deploy, so these are
         uncommented in the commit that first reads them, together with the account resource named
         beside each. Names carry the environment for the same reason the queue does — every one of
         these namespaces is account-global.

         "analytics_engine_datasets": [
           { "binding": "TELEMETRY", "dataset": "braindog_telemetry_alpha" }],
         "ratelimits": [
           { "name": "RL_READ",  "namespace_id": "1001", "simple": { "limit": 600, "period": 60 } },
           { "name": "RL_WRITE", "namespace_id": "1002", "simple": { "limit": 120, "period": 60 } }],
         "secrets_store_secrets": [
           { "binding": "KEK",      "store_id": "<store-id>", "secret_name": "braindog-kek-alpha" },
           { "binding": "KEK_PREV", "store_id": "<store-id>", "secret_name": "braindog-kek-alpha-prev" }]

         THERE IS NO `kv_namespaces` KEY IN EITHER ENVIRONMENT, AND THERE WILL NOT BE ONE. The
         record is the Durable Object's own SQLite (33 §7); the Directory is a Durable Object; the
         shell is `assets`; sessions, tokens and lockouts are rows in the Directory (33 §10). There
         is nothing left for a KV namespace to hold, and an eventually-consistent store beside a
         single-writer object is a second answer to questions that already have one.
         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    },

    /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       PRODUCTION — script `braindog-production`. Deployed from a tag, or by hand from
       `workflow_dispatch`, behind the `production` GitHub Environment's approval.

       NOTE THE SCRIPT NAME. A named environment deploys `<name>-<environment>`, so declaring these
       two environments RENAMES the production Worker from `braindog` to `braindog-production`. If
       `braindog` has ever been deployed, its Durable Objects stay with `braindog` and the new
       script starts empty — this is a migration, not a rename, and REPORT.md §8 risk R1 is the
       one thing to settle before the first run of `deploy.yml`.
       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    "production": {
      /* THE RECORD IS ON `app.brain.dog` AND THE APEX IS THE MARKETING SITE. 51 §4.6 puts the
         Worker on `brain.dog` with `PUBLIC_HOST: "brain.dog"`, which cannot hold: `apps/site`
         serves `/`, `/pricing` and `/changelog` from the same apex (14 §4), and the Worker's
         `single-page-application` fallback answers every unmatched path with the app shell — so
         `brain.dog/pricing` would resolve as a spoken id and draw "no such entry". One apex, one
         owner. REPORT.md §1 sets out the split and the sentences it costs (19 §5's `brain.dog/D41`
         becomes `app.brain.dog/D41`); amend 51 §4.6 and 19 §5. */
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "app.brain.dog", "custom_domain": true }],

      "vars": {
        "PUBLIC_HOST": "app.brain.dog",
        "AI_GATEWAY": "braindog",
        "AGE_RECIPIENT": "age1<production-recipient>",
        "SPARK_ENDPOINT": "",
        "BRAINDOG_BUILD": "",
        "DEMO_RECORD": "",
        /* EMPTY IS NO SIGN-IN LINKS. The sender, on a domain Email Service sends for, switches them on
           with the `EMAIL` binding below (51 §5.8). */
        "AUTH_MAIL_FROM": "",
        /* AND THE ONE WORD THAT LETS A KEYLESS RECORD BE MADE ON PURPOSE. Empty is the default and
           the default is a refusal: with no `KEK` bound, `/auth/provision` answers
           `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` rather than making a record that can never seal a grant, because a
           record is given its keys once and the moment is the create. `yes` and nothing else turns
           that off, for a host that seals nothing. Set here or with
           `--var PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS:yes` on the deploy, as `DEMO_RECORD` above is set. */
        "PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS": ""
      },

      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [
          { "name": "RECORD", "class_name": "Record$" },
          { "name": "DIRECTORY", "class_name": "Directory$" }
        ]
      },

      "queues": {
        "producers": [{ "queue": "braindog-ingress", "binding": "INGRESS" }],
        "consumers": [
          {
            "queue": "braindog-ingress",
            "max_batch_size": 25,
            "max_batch_timeout": 5,
            "max_retries": 3
          }
        ]
      },

      "send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],

      /* REPEATED IN FULL, because `r2_buckets` is not inheritable and an omitted non-inheritable
         key is EMPTY rather than inherited: production would deploy, come up with no bucket, and
         the first sign of it would be `scheduled()` throwing at 03:17. The bucket must exist before
         this deploy: `wrangler r2 bucket create braindog-blobs`. */
      "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "BLOBS", "bucket_name": "braindog-blobs" }],

      /* REPEATED IN FULL, for the reason `r2_buckets` above is: `ai` is not inheritable, and an
         omitted non-inheritable key is empty rather than inherited. Production without this line
         deploys, comes up, and answers `ModelUnavailable` to the first ask Spark makes. The
         argument for uncommenting it is in `alpha` above; there is no account resource to create
         for it, unlike every block still commented below. */
      "ai": { "binding": "AI" }

      /* The same drafted blocks as alpha, without the suffix:
         "analytics_engine_datasets": [
           { "binding": "TELEMETRY", "dataset": "braindog_telemetry" }],
         "ratelimits": [
           { "name": "RL_READ",  "namespace_id": "1001", "simple": { "limit": 600, "period": 60 } },
           { "name": "RL_WRITE", "namespace_id": "1002", "simple": { "limit": 120, "period": 60 } }],
         "secrets_store_secrets": [
           { "binding": "KEK",      "store_id": "<store-id>", "secret_name": "braindog-kek" },
           { "binding": "KEK_PREV", "store_id": "<store-id>", "secret_name": "braindog-kek-prev" }]  */
    }
  }

  /* WORKER SECRETS ARE NOT IN THIS FILE AND NEVER WILL BE. `SESSION_SECRET` and
     `TOKENS_VALID_FROM` (12 §8's two fleet-wide levers, 53 §5.11's incident response) are set with
     `wrangler secret put <NAME> --env <environment>`, value at the prompt and never as an
     argument (53 §2.2). They are PER SCRIPT, so alpha and production hold different values —
     which is the point: a shared `SESSION_SECRET` would make a session minted in alpha valid in
     production, since the session hash is an HMAC under it.

     SIGN-IN SHIPS SWITCHED OFF, AND THESE ARE ITS SWITCHES. Without `SESSION_SECRET` (32 characters or
     more) `/auth/methods` says `not-configured` and every other `/auth` route is 404. `PROVISION_KEY`
     is the third secret: the operator's bearer for `/auth/provision` and `/auth/claim/mint`, which make a
     record and hand back its 24-hour claim link; without it both are 404.

     `KEK` IS THE FOURTH, AND THE KEY DOORS' SWITCH (12 §6, 51 §8 rule 3). Its name is declared here and its value
     never is: 32 bytes as 64 hex characters or base64url, a different value per environment, put at the prompt with
     `wrangler secret put KEK --env <environment>`, or bound from Secrets Store by the drafted
     `secrets_store_secrets` block above under the same name, never both. Without it `/api/vault/*` answers
     `NOT_IN_THIS_BUILD` after checking the grant, and `/auth/provision` REFUSES rather than making a keyless record.
     With it, records provisioned from then on get their three keys, and one made before keeps none. Amend 12 §6,
     51 §8 rule 3 and 16 ADR-062 in the same revision: all three said provisioning carried on without keys, which
     was true until the guard in `src/keyless.ts` landed. The reason for the change is that the mistake cannot be
     undone — a record created with no `vault_key` rows never gets them, because a second create would overwrite the
     DEK every sealed grant was made under — so the only repair is to throw the Directory name away, and 52 §13 has
     no undo for one. `PROVISION_WITHOUT_KEYS` in `vars` is the deliberate exception. It is deliberately not
     listed under `secrets.required`: that key fails the first deploy of an environment while the secret is unset,
     and unset is a supported state for every door but that one. `KEK_PREV` is not read yet. */
}
